# Expected error-class log lines for genesis-fork-round.sh.
#
# THE ALLOWLIST SHRANK WHEN THE FIX LANDED, and that direction is the point. While
# the node could not adopt a genesis-rooted chain, this file excused the wedge's own
# diagnostics as "the round's evidence". Now that adoption is required, those lines
# are regressions again and are deliberately NOT listed:
#
#   "declining a range rooted at GENESIS"     BlockFetch refused the switch
#   "cannot rejoin the network"               the latched escalation
#   "beyond forecast horizon"                 the park that follows a stalled ledger
#
# Keeping them here would have made the round pass whether the node adopted or
# wedged — a check that cannot fail, which is the defect class this file's own
# history is a case study in.
#
# Kept deliberately NARROW for the same reason. A pattern like "ERROR" would swallow
# the ledger-rollback failure that distinguishes a BAD fix (storage emits a
# genesis-rooted plan the ledger cannot execute) from a good one.
#
# Format: one extended-regex per line; '#' comments and blank lines ignored.

# The frozen island's forge loop cannot build a ledger view for the current slot,
# because step 2b deliberately parks dugite-bp ~300 slots behind and SIGSTOPs it.
# Expected on dugite-bp for the whole freeze and reconnect window.
TraceNoLedgerView

# Steps 2b/2c/3/5 stop or freeze nodes and swap topologies, so their peers
# legitimately observe the disconnect and re-dial.
removed dead connection
shutting down peer connection

# NOT allowlisted, on purpose — these must fail the round if they appear:
#   "Fork rollback failed"                         (the BAD-FIX shape: storage
#                                                   emitted a genesis-rooted plan
#                                                   the ledger could not execute)
#   "Rollback target outside LedgerSeq"            (same, one layer down)
#   "LedgerSeq was incoherent"                     (#985's chimera)
#
# NOTE FOR ANY FUTURE ENTRY: the parser treats a line starting with '#' as a COMMENT
# (`case "$pat" in ''|\#*) continue ;;`), so a pattern beginning with an issue
# number like "#1057: ..." is silently DROPPED and the line it was meant to allow
# then fails the round. That happened here once. Never start a pattern with '#'.
